TRACE={
'm8c05':('Why can nutrient inputs lead to an oxygen crash, and which measurements give warning?','From C04 and G02, the learner can read standards and select the right guideline. Extra limiting nutrient removes a growth brake; algae grow; dead material feeds microbial respiration; oxygen falls; the student chooses temperature, nutrients and algal counts for warning. The matched lake states and day/night trace serve this chain. The spatial table needs the inference repair above.','No re-teaching of DO measurement or treatment processes is needed. The one treatment link stays. C04 colourimetry is a brief recall.'),
'm8c06':('What do DO and BOD measure, and how does a Winkler titre become mg/L?','From C05 and C01, the learner starts with oxygen demand and cV. Fixing links oxygen to brown manganese solid, acid releases iodine, thiosulfate measures iodine, the 1:4 ratio converts the titre, and mass/sample volume produces 6.40 mg/L. The later five-day comparison needs a fixed-amount meaning.','C05 day/night swings and warm water are recalls. C01 titration arithmetic is applied to a new ratio, not unnecessarily repeated.'),
'm8c08':('Which candidate functional groups do visible reactions support?','From M7 P02/P04/C09, the learner knows the reactions. The new task is interpreting a set of positive and negative results, preserving multiple groups and admitting what remains unresolved. The candidate strip and full three-test tree serve this new decision, once the selectivity assumption is removed.','Scenes 3-5 repeat familiar mechanisms briefly. Preserve the tubes and causal recalls, but do not re-run P02 or P04 as a full practical here.'),
'm8p05c':('How can a student plan, control and evaluate functional-group tests and support each claim?','From C08, P02, P04, P03 and S07, the learner can choose blank/known/unknown, hold method conditions constant, record observation before inference, and evaluate a contaminated blank. The unknown D comparison makes a negative result concrete. Exact alkene selectivity and safe gas setup need repair.','P02/P04 quantities can remain on recalled method cards. S07 terminology is applied to this method. The new learning is control quality and defensible inference, not another mechanism explanation.'),
'm8b09':('Why do most changed cells avoid cancer, and how can melanoma invade and spread?','From mitosis and somatic mutation, the learner follows damage protection, several driver changes in one lineage, activation of growth signalling, loss of brakes, invasion, vessel entry and a secondary melanoma deposit. The clone tree and vessel sequence are purposeful. The distinction between harmless mutation, repairable damage and a driver must come first.','B06 classified cancer and B07 explained UV damage. Keep each as one recall. The full four-stage mitosis recap can be cut to fund the corrected A1. Melanoma remains the approved main case.'),
'm8b10':('What do incidence, prevalence and mortality measure, and how can counts and rates disagree?','The learner knows B06-B09 disease cases and S05 calculation layout. In the same city, changing only the numerator produces 200 new diagnoses, 5,500 existing cases and 20 deaths per 100,000. The tap/tank then explains accumulation, and age standardisation explains the hook after the graph is sourced.','S05 contributes the equation/substitute/solve/check habit, not a duplicate epidemiology lesson. G01 is later application. Graph-selection reminders largely overlap S06 and can be trimmed.'),
'm8b11':('How should a student judge an epidemiological method and its benefit?','From B10 rates and B07 exposure risk, the learner starts with 1950 disease groups looking back, then a 1951 cohort looking forward, then several causal evidence lines. The four-step method frame is applied to hair dye, and the benefit chain transfers to fortification. Historical timeline and endpoint labels need the listed repairs.','B10 leaves study design to this lesson. B07 mechanism and Australian smoking trends are brief recalls. G01 follows this lesson and applies the vocabulary; no need to move G01 back.'),
'm8g01b':('How do I calculate a rate, draw rate data and judge a study claim?','From B10, B11, S05 and S06, the learner divides by the correct population, constructs a scaled time graph, then evaluates a cross-sectional energy-drink survey. Full givens remain through each countdown, and walkthroughs are the content, so their longer feedback is appropriate.','S05 and S06 routines are used on new epidemiology data. B11 is genuinely needed for confounding, temporality, cohort and the four-part judgement. Cross-sectional gets a short new definition before it is used.')}
